  "body": "Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" The two reply texts are, first, a fenced block containing two JSON objects followed by an unfenced object: ```json\n{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}\n```\n{\"c\":3}; and second, just the unfenced object: {\"c\":3}. For the first reply, the rule requires the recovered object to be {\"b\":2}, because it is the last object inside the code fence. For the second reply, the rule requires {\"c\":3}, because there is no fence and it is the last top-level object. The function returns {\"c\":3} for both: in the first reply the fenced pool candidate is the entire fenced content `{\"a\":1} {\"b\":2}`, which fails to parse as a single object, so the function falls through to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object, which is the unfenced {\"c\":3}. This makes the two replies indistinguishable to the check. I would be wrong if the function returned {\"b\":2} for the first reply, or if the documented rule were read as treating the entire fenced content as a single candidate that, when it fails to parse, means no fenced object holds one and the last top-level object is correctly used.",
